  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nWith the wolfSSL backend, when CA caching is enabled and an `CURLOPT_SSL_CTX_FUNCTION` callback replaces the trust store, curl can silently reinstall the cached store after the callback returns. A certificate trusted by the cached store but rejected by the callback-selected store is then incorrectly accepted.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`Curl_wssl_setup_x509_store()` in `lib/vtls/wolfssl.c` installs a cached X509 store but does not set `wssl->x509_store_setup = TRUE`. That flag is only set inside `wssl_populate_x509_store()`, which runs on cache misses.\n\nThe sequence on a cache hit:\n\n1. Before invoking the callback, `wssl_connect_step1()` calls `Curl_wssl_setup_x509_store()` — cached store A is installed, flag stays `false`.\n2. The `CURLOPT_SSL_CTX_FUNCTION` callback runs and replaces store A with store B.\n3. During the handshake, the still-false flag causes `Curl_wssl_setup_x509_store()` to run again. It finds `CTX_get_cert_store() != cached_store` (the callback changed it), so it reinstalls cached store A.\n\n## Affected versions\n\nIntroduced in commit `0f2876b2c33f` (Jul 2024, \"wolfSSL: CA store share fix\" for #14278). Present in releases **8.9.1 through 8.21.0**.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn application that uses `CURLOPT_SSL_CTX_FUNCTION` to restrict trust (e.g. pin to a private CA) has that restriction silently bypassed when CA caching is active. A MITM presenting a certificate valid under the cached store but rejected by the callback's store succeeds.\n\nPrerequisites: wolfSSL backend, `CURLOPT_CA_CACHE_TIMEOUT != 0`, a callback that replaces the store, and differing trust between the two stores.\n\n## Reproduction\n1. Build an affected curl release (8.9.1 through 8.21.0) against wolfSSL and configure curl without a compiled-in default CA path so that a CA file is eligible for caching.\n2. Create two independent certificate authorities, called CA A and CA B.\n3. Create a TLS server certificate for the test hostname, including a matching subject alternative name, and sign it with CA A.\n4. Start an HTTPS server on the test hostname using that certificate.\n5. Write a small libcurl client that uses the same multi handle for two transfers, sets `CURLOPT_CAINFO` to CA A, and sets a nonzero `CURLOPT_CA_CACHE_TIMEOUT`.\n6. Make the first transfer without an SSL-context callback. Confirm that it succeeds and thereby populates curl's CA cache with the trust store containing CA A.\n7. For the second transfer, use a fresh connection and register `CURLOPT_SSL_CTX_FUNCTION`. In the callback, replace the wolfSSL context's certificate store with a newly created store containing only CA B.\n8. Disable connection reuse and TLS session caching for both transfers so that the second transfer performs a new TLS handshake and certificate verification.\n9. Make the second transfer to the same HTTPS server. On a vulnerable build, it incorrectly succeeds even though the callback-selected store contains only CA B and the server certificate chains only to CA A.\n10. Repeat the sequence with `CURLOPT_CA_CACHE_TIMEOUT` set to zero. The second transfer should now fail certificate verification, demonstrating that the unexpected acceptance depends on the CA-cache path.\n11. As a fixed-version check, repeat the cached sequence on a build containing commit `ed0338befd` or a later release. The second transfer should fail because curl no longer reinstalls CA A after the callback selects CA B.\n\nThe callback-selected trust store must remain authoritative. The transfer using store B should reject the server certificate regardless of whether curl's CA cache was previously populated.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nSet `wssl->x509_store_setup = TRUE` in `Curl_wssl_setup_x509_store()` itself (before returning), rather than only inside `wssl_populate_x509_store()`. This ensures the flag is set on both cache hits and misses, preventing the second invocation from overwriting the callback's store.\n\n## Impact\n\n## Summary:\nAn attacker able to present a hostname-valid certificate trusted by curl’s cached CA store—but rejected by the application-selected store—can impersonate the intended TLS endpoint. This bypasses the application’s trust policy and may allow interception or modification of encrypted traffic.",
